<p>REVEAL 1 — Different perspectives. Different stakes.</p>
<p>We have people here at very different stages and in very different roles—medical students, trainees, junior and senior faculty, and colleagues in industry. We’re coming to this conversation for different reasons. Some are learning how to contribute to their first paper. Others are leading collaborations or managing publication programs. What we need from this discussion will differ.</p>
<p>REVEAL 2 — Shared work. Different expectations.</p>
<p>But we often work together on the same science. And we may bring different expectations about what earns authorship, who should be invited to contribute, and when those conversations should happen. Those differences can stay invisible until a manuscript is nearly finished.</p>
<p>REVEAL 3 — Criteria give us a starting point.</p>
<p>We have guidelines, and we’ll start with those. But applying them requires judgment: what counts as a substantial contribution, what happens when someone joins late, and how should expectations change as the work evolves?</p>
<p>REVEAL 4 — A conversation we can learn from.</p>
<p>That’s why we’re having a workshop. We’ll use the cases and survey to hear how others approach these questions, understand where our expectations differ, and learn from one another. You don’t need extensive publishing experience to contribute—your questions and perspective are part of the conversation.</p>
