## What does a meaningful path look like? {#module-1-path-problem .aoa-slide data-aoa-section="case-1"}

<div class="aoa-shell"><div class="aoa-topbar"><div class="aoa-section-label">CASE 01 · OPTIONAL BACKUP</div><div class="aoa-heading">What does a meaningful path look like?</div></div><div class="aoa-content"><div class="revamp-agenda"><div class="revamp-agenda-item fragment fade-in" data-fragment-index="0"><div class="revamp-number">01</div><div><div class="revamp-item-title">The manuscript is already finished.</div><div class="revamp-item-copy">“Please review and approve.”</div></div></div><div class="revamp-agenda-item fragment fade-in" data-fragment-index="1"><div class="revamp-number">02</div><div><div class="revamp-item-title">But what can the contributor still shape?</div><div class="revamp-item-copy">The analysis? The interpretation? The scientific story?</div></div></div><div class="revamp-agenda-item fragment fade-in" data-fragment-index="2"><div class="revamp-number">03</div><div><div class="revamp-item-title">An invitation needs room for contribution.</div><div class="revamp-item-copy">What would make this a meaningful path to qualify?</div></div></div></div></div></div><aside class="notes"><p>Optional backup for the discussion with Lisa. Respondents distinguish clinical care from an opportunity to qualify. Use the actual distribution on the preceding slide; the percentage can change as the survey refreshes.</p><p>That raises the practical question: what is the path? For many of us, the invitation arrives with a manuscript that is essentially done. We are asked to read it and give it a thumbs-up.</p><p>Substantive intellectual revision can still matter at that stage. But how much room have we actually left for the clinician to question the analysis, change the interpretation, or shape the story?</p><p>Let me show you one approach we tried—not as the answer, but as a concrete example for discussion.</p></aside>

## Office hours {#module-1-office-hours .aoa-slide data-aoa-section="case-1"}

<div class="aoa-shell"><div class="aoa-topbar"><div class="aoa-section-label">CASE 01 · OPTIONAL BACKUP</div><div class="aoa-heading">Office hours</div></div><div class="aoa-content"><div class="revamp-agenda"><div class="revamp-agenda-item fragment fade-in" data-fragment-index="0"><div class="revamp-number">01</div><div><div class="revamp-item-title">Invite clinicians into the project.</div><div class="revamp-item-copy">Start with their connection to the patient cohort.</div></div></div><div class="revamp-agenda-item fragment fade-in" data-fragment-index="1"><div class="revamp-number">02</div><div><div class="revamp-item-title">Make the methods visible.</div><div class="revamp-item-copy">Patient identification · abstraction · analysis · preliminary findings</div></div></div><div class="revamp-agenda-item fragment fade-in" data-fragment-index="2"><div class="revamp-number">03</div><div><div class="revamp-item-title">Create a place to contribute.</div><div class="revamp-item-copy">Brief office hours for questions, interpretation, and refinement.</div></div></div><div class="revamp-agenda-item fragment fade-in" data-fragment-index="3"><div class="revamp-number">04</div><div><div class="revamp-item-title">Let people choose to engage.</div><div class="revamp-item-copy">Offer several sessions and a clear, low-pressure invitation.</div></div></div></div><div class="case-one-principle fragment fade-in" data-fragment-index="4">An opportunity to contribute. Authorship still requires the full criteria.</div></div></div><aside class="notes"><p>In one project, we identified potential contributors through their involvement in patient care. Rather than only circulating a finished manuscript, I invited interested colleagues to a brief office-hour session.</p><p>We offered several dates and asked about their familiarity with analytical concepts so the discussion could be tailored. The sessions were designed to explain how patients were identified, how abstraction and analyses were performed, and what the preliminary findings showed.</p><p>The invitation was to ask questions, engage with the data, strengthen interpretation, and identify what might need refinement. Colleagues could opt in without pressure if the project fit their interests and bandwidth.</p><p>Attendance was an entry point to engagement, not an authorship credential by itself. The important part was the opportunity to contribute intellectually and then meet the full authorship criteria.</p><p>Optional personal context, if you want to use your recollection: roughly 50 colleagues were invited and about 11 engaged. These are approximate recollections, not a verified participation analysis. They are intentionally not displayed on the slide.</p><p>Optional backup: explain the project orally if you use this example. This is one approach we tried. What did it make possible—and what would others do differently?</p></aside>

